# 1938-1940: A Diamond-Hard History
Tamil title: ஒரு வச்சிர வரலாறு

## Preface

Is there a law that history must concern only realms, kings, and wars? No. Therefore, a family history that occurred during the period from 1938 to 1940 is engraved here. It is not the history of a royal family, but of common folk.

## Evidence

Does this history have sources and evidence? Histories customarily rely upon inscriptions, epics, legends, buried objects, and many such proofs. This particular history likewise has sources and evidence: a few sentences found in three records.

1. The expense ledger of commission-shop owner Sokkalingam Chettiar.
2. A lady doctor's diary.
3. A birth-register entry.

The history will now be drawn. Read it and learn its lesson; also see the picture with your mind's eye.

## 1938

Kannusami is worth his weight in gold: gentle and trustworthy. I have entrusted the entire responsibility for the shop to him. It seems Kannusami must be the one who wakes the rooster each morning! He rises so early, bathes, has a view of the temple tower, runs home, takes the bunch of keys, opens the shop, sweeps and cleans it himself, and arranges the seat upon which I sit. He attends to his work so diligently that I can rise leisurely at eight, clean my teeth, bathe, finish my worship of Vinayaka, eat my tiffin at ease, and reach the shop peacefully at nine or half past nine.

The bazaar has any number of clipped moustaches and idle legs, but one cannot find another boy like our Kannusami. Why speak of how I brought him up? Many people imagine that he is my son or at least a very close relation. He belongs to some Naidu community, whereas I am Sokkalingam Chetti. Just see! Somehow the boy has such affection and love for me. I have no son, only one daughter, and she is a widow. After me, Kannusami must inherit the shop. God cannot fail to help a man of his worth and conduct.

Let us suppose that something like this had been written in the 1938 diary of Sokkalingam Chettiar, who owned a commission shop. Why do I say “let us suppose”? Because he really ought to have written it, but did not. In place of all this, a single sentence appeared in his expense ledger:

> Kannusami's salary increased by five rupees.

What was written above is only part of the full meaning of that sentence. Could he fill page after page of a shop daybook and ledger with praises such as “worth his weight in gold,” “gentle,” “diligent,” and “watches the shop's affairs with eye and mind”? “Kannusami's salary increased by five rupees” is a rare prose poem containing all those praises and more. In one second, it awakened joy in an entire household.

Do you know what Adinarayana Naidu would have had to write in a diary for 1938?

“Lord! By your gracious glance I was born a son of prosperity. I served you to the best of my ability. People blamed you because my family wealth was ruined by the palanquin festival I conducted every year for the Padmanabha temple. Let others' blame pass; even I grew weary and disgusted, asking what use gods or spirits were. I was overcome by grief that house and property were gone and I had been reduced to nothing.

“Kannusami was born after I had visited countless temples and given so much in charity. Because the times changed for me, he now works as a shop clerk. I do not know whether he rises at four or five. When I open my eyes, he is already wearing the Vaishnavite mark and shining with divine radiance. May he live long! He labours day and night to earn fifteen rupees and keeps not even a farthing for himself. Meenakshi finds a special joy in receiving that money.

“Has she never seen money? I once tossed bundles of notes before her, and Meena put them away in the box. She was accustomed to handling money. Even so, the fifteen rupees our son brings seems like ten thousand to her. 'Kannu brought home his pay today,' she sings as though it were a song. Naturally! Could the notes I used to throw before her have delighted her when they were not money I had earned? I would sell a house, garden, wet field, jewel, or something else, spend most of the proceeds, and give Meenakshi what remained. How could that make her happy?

“But Kannu's fifteen rupees were earned by his labour. They breathe life into the family. That money has kept me from begging in a broad religious mark while singing devotional songs. Is it only his mother and I who praise Kannusami's character? Astrologer Gopal himself has said that if he lives long, all the wealth I lost will return.

“It is because his employer recognized my son's good character that he has raised the salary by five rupees from this month. May he live like a king! From the twenty rupees he now pays, we must set aside at least two each month for Kannusami's marriage. Kannu is twenty-two. At that age I already knew what particular 'varieties' were available at the great holy places. I was such a wastrel. Kannu is wise and does not stray into wasteful ways. You must protect him and make him prosper further.

“May shop owner Sokkalingam Chettiar always remain as faithful to Kannu as he is now. May his trade and profit grow. He has abundant money but no peace of mind, poor man. His only daughter, lovely as a parrot, has become a widow. Kannu says streams of tears flow from the man's eyes whenever he thinks of Kalyani. What can be done? Each person's fate is their own.”

Adinarayana Naidu ought necessarily to have written all this in a diary. Instead, at the shrine of Mother Pankajavalli, he arranged one offering in Kannusami's name and another in Sokkalingam Chettiar's name and brought back the sacred food. Those two offerings contained thoughts deeper than notes filling seven or eight pages.

In 1938 Kalyani had nothing she could write. How could that young woman put unbearable grief into writing? Where were the letters for it? The volcano burning within the flower-like woman who had lost her own flower belched fire. How could she gather into words what had entered her heart?

For one instant the whole world appeared to her as a city of injustice inhabited by ignorance. At times the world itself seemed a widow that had lost its husband, Happiness. Every man and woman who danced, sang, and enjoyed life appeared to Kalyani as an enemy of long standing, a sinner who mocked her to take revenge.

How could the sorrow of eighteen-year-old Kalyani ever subside when her husband died of chest pain in the third month of their marriage? Her mind's suffering cannot be told. Painter or epic poet would fail in any attempt to depict that portrait of grief. Kalyani was not greatly educated, whether at school or in the world.

“Kannusami has a heart of gold. My husband's grief has damaged even his skill in business. When the telegram came saying that Kalyani's husband had died, he wailed until it seemed his very brain would shatter. 'Whom have I harmed that this thunderbolt should strike my head? Did I forget service to God? Did I go about speaking lies? My only daughter has become a widow! Why do I need house or property now? For whom are the shop and its accounts? I shall simply become a wanderer. As the saying goes, let the cremation ground be my house and the begging bowl my possession.'

“When he wept so, it was Kannusami who consoled him and looked after house and shop. Without the boy's aid and attention, my husband would have died. The poor lad cares so much for our family. One's own sons squander family property and become worthless, but is Kannu of our caste or clan? No. Even so, our relatives possess not a mustard seed of the love he bears our family. Our relations are filled with jealousy and evil thoughts. Honey flows when they speak before us, but behind our backs they talk wildly. Such afflictions are called our kin! They are not equal even to the dust beneath Kannusami's feet!”

Seethalakshmi Ammal did not write this in a diary. She had no diary. Even if one had been found, what then? She did not know how to write. But thought can arise without literacy. Every day she wrote her unwritten diary through affectionate words:

“Brother, do not go yet. Eat just this one dosa before you leave.”

“Why are you up so early, Kannu? What shall we do if you develop a fever?”

“I sent *porivilangai* sweets to the shop. Did you eat them, or did you forget even that in your work?”

Kannusami's diary for 1938 must have been brief: obey the employer; live mindful of the town's opinion; protect the family; earn a name as a respectable man in the bazaar. This was the four-line moral text Kannusami recited.

To his eyes, employer Sokkalingam Chettiar appeared “a pitiable, charitable, utterly gentle man.” Seethalakshmi Ammal was a mother to him. And Kalyani? He had never properly looked at her. Yes, precisely because he had not looked, matters in 1938 allowed us to say that his diary must have read this way.

## 1939

What a change between the world of 1938 and that of 1939! Kingdoms destroyed, kings driven from palaces, treaties broken, statesmen exhausted, dreams faded, glorious cities burned: so many things! Newspapers informed the public, and people understood from the change in life's condition that they lived and suffered amid a terrible war. They knew of the disorders of kingdoms, the distress of governments, the destruction of goods, and their causes.

Could the people of Madras lack newspapers? At five every evening, the whole world came and stood before their eyes. Editors existed to render that very service. While Madras busied itself learning the state of the world, would it care about commission-shop owner Sokkalingam Chettiar's notebook for 1939?

Yet Sokkalingam's bewilderment at the change in Kannusami's conduct was no smaller than the public's bewilderment at world affairs. Kannusami did not lift cannon and rifle to shatter the Chettiar's mind. His invasion was conducted without knife or blood. Who seeks a sword when eyes are available in the war of love, especially when Kalyani was not blind and Kannusami knew no Tirukkural? Had not the author of that work observed many Kalyanis and Kannusamis before writing:

> When eye meets eye in equal gaze,
> what use can spoken words possess?

Sokkalingam Chettiar caught them at the moment when eye met eye. He was shocked and bewildered. Why had our golden Kannusami changed? Had his spotless mind become stained? he grieved.

But did Kannusami not grieve too? Such beauty, at the age in which beauty resides! Limbs filled with youthful loveliness, borne by a golden vine! And such a Kalyani had no husband. A widow! As though drawing a curtain across the moon or sealing musk and perfume inside a casket, they had shut Kalyani's beauty within the box called “restriction.” Would desire lie like a snake imprisoned in a box? It bored a little hole beneath the box and escaped.

Despite wealth and a form like a golden image, Kalyani had no husband. Were there no men in the world? Any man who saw Kalyani and felt nothing must surely be blind.

Kannusami too grew sorrowful: “The more I see her, the more my mind resembles a fever patient's as his temperature rises toward evening. They say a man begins to rave when the fever peaks and delirium sets in. I fear I shall become like that.”

At rare moments Kalyani's smile worked as a treatment. At all other times he tried and failed to subdue that monkey of a mind. Kalyani was why his attention to shop work declined, why his body began to grow thin, and why his employer's anger came to fall upon him.

In place of a diary, Chettiar wrote his customary prose poem, leaving those who knew its meaning to understand:

> Two rupees to be deducted
> from Kannusami's salary.

Adinarayana Naidu contented himself by saying, “Somehow the times are unfavourable.” Seethalakshmi tried acting as counsel: “Has the loss of those two rupees caused you some tremendous expense? Why pounce on the poor boy?” The case was dismissed, and there was no court of appeal.

Kannusami, virtuous in 1938, had in 1939, within a single year, become a rogue who betrayed the “charitable patron” who fed him. Poor Kalyani had tried to restrain the feelings of youth by saying and thinking that many widows in the town shared her fate. Within that same year she became, in the world's judgement, a perverse woman capable of arguing:

“What am I to do? I tried as hard as I could and failed. What is surprising in this? When jasmine blossoms on the vine, a traveller on the road enjoys its fragrance; what can the jasmine do about it? Why did you allow Kannusami to wander here? Can you release a magnet and then order the iron to obey a law? Can you pluck a lute and command that no music arise? Only a fool would do so!”

What indeed could she do, poor woman? Sokkalingam Chettiar had reached an age at which desire steadily faded, so thoughts of worldly illusion, life's impermanence, and the disgrace brought by sensual passion could take up residence in his mind. Kalyani possessed a wave-tossed heart at an age incapable of restraining it, and the waves grew more violent every day. Was that her fault? No, no! What could she do? Youth made her submit.

Kannusami first appeared to her as the visible shop clerk. Then he took the form of “someone very important to Father,” appeared morning and evening in the guise of a devotee bearing fruit or flowers from the shop, and at last revealed himself as a lover. She had not seized a passer-by and dragged him in from the street. Her father sent him to the place where she lived. He came as spring's messenger and found a place in that beautiful woman's heart. What use is it to blame her?

She remembered clearly everything that had happened during the year.

In 1938 he had been golden and gentle; in 1939 he was a wastrel and scoundrel. That was Chettiar's account. By his reckoning, Kannusami, trustworthy in 1938, had become treacherous in 1939. The very man who had praised him as intelligent now called him a fool. The Kannusami whom he had once described as an innocent who knew nothing was now abused as a schemer and hypocrite. The man who lovingly invited him in with “Come inside, brother” in 1938 drove him away in 1939 with “Get out, you donkey! Stay outside!” Thus had he changed. Kalyani too had changed in the same way. Change is only natural.

At first she had asked why she should appear before a man. Chettiar himself reassured her: “Silly girl! There is no stranger here, only our Kannusami.” She never spoke to Kannusami, but Chettiar himself created the habit. Sitting at the shop, he would send Kannusami home with messages: “Ask Kalyani what savoury snack she wants and bring word back.”

At first she meant to say only yes or no and send him away. But his gaze held affection, and his speech contained an intoxicating drug. Kalyani's heart and face began to blossom. What could she do? Ask her and you will know who was at fault.

Leave Kalyani aside and question her eyes: “How could you desire this wrongdoing? Is lust proper? Why did you pour love upon Kannusami?” The eyes cannot speak, but one or two pearls will fall. Do you know what those tears mean?

“Mad world! Vindictive world! Did I suddenly leap upon that golden figure? Today you see desire overflowing within me. At first I was angry because this Kannusami repeatedly looked at me in a certain way. He paid no heed to that anger, and I poured out still more: how dare he? It did not scorch him. He continued bathing me in his affection. I thought, poor man, he seems ready for whatever may come because his longing is so great. Pity arose in me. He made use of that feeling. I poured fear upon him, but he looked at me with a smile. Shame! Do I not possess even one-thousandth of his courage? Am I a coward? That thought arose, and temptation entered. Temptation soon brought love to dwell within me. Now longing overflows. Without knowing this, you condemn me, hard-hearted world!

“Do you know how many struggles a widow's eyes endured before they made room for desire? What do you know? Even if you knew, you would remain mute. You have a mouth only to ask questions; when the need comes to answer them, you fall silent. What have I to say to you?”

That was the meaning of her tears. Those who understood, understood; many did not, and they were of no use. Seeing Kalyani's hand wipe those eyes, people would repeat their usual vulgarity: “The sly woman rubs her eyes to put on a show!” They did not know that her hand was not merely wiping her eyes. It gently stroked and comforted them by relating its own suffering:

“Do not falter, dear eyes! You imagine this suffering is yours alone. Foolish things, do you know what I endured? Listen to the story. One night he touched me without warning. The hand that pushed him away later became a red flag indicating that Mother was inside. Later still it covered my mouth to signal, 'Come silently,' gestured, 'Do not go; stay, I shall return at once,' threatened playfully, 'Just see what I shall do to you,' then embraced him and found delight. Today it strokes my lower belly in distress. Do only you know that story? Does the world?”

Thus Kalyani's hands rendered the service of consoling her eyes. To the world's gaze, it merely appeared that a hand rubbed an eye. The world! What does it know of the heart?

“It sounds as if some man is coming,” Kalyani once said fearfully.

It was Chettiar himself who replied, “There is no stranger, child! It is our Kannu. Silly girl, why feel shy before him? He is our shop boy!”

Only after that reassurance had grown did Kalyani, hearing footsteps near the threshold, answer before Sokkalingam finished asking “Who is coming?”: “Who else could it be? It must be our Kannusami.” The time had arrived when even his footfall began giving pleasure to her ears. What could she do?

At the next stage Kannusami came silently like a cat and dallied with his graceful beloved, then slipped away when he heard another person. When her mother asked, “I heard someone come; who was it?” Kalyani claimed him as her own in answering, “No one, Mother, only our Kannusami.”

Was Kalyani responsible for all this? Was not Chettiar, who watched it develop but lacked the ability to prevent such a situation from arising, responsible? Kalyani did not surrender at the first touch, nor did Kannusami fall in love at first sight and drag her away by the hand. A fierce struggle continued in both minds for many nights.

The right side of Kalyani's pillow preached into her ear: “You are a widow! You have lost your *thali*, remember? Do not fall into wrongdoing. It will bring disgrace not only upon you but upon the whole family.”

The voice from the left said: “Your beauty and youth are being wasted. You wither without tasting youth's feast. Your husband died; what could you do about that? Did the dead man carry away your feelings and allure? No! Comb your hair and look at your beauty in the standing mirror. Offer it to Kannusami. He loves you sincerely.”

With moral instruction on the right and a messenger of pleasure on the left, the pillow conducted a battle against Kalyani for many nights. She refused to turn either way and lay flat on her back so that she need hear neither voice. Did that end it? No. Pictures on the bedroom wall showed a god enthroned with his goddess and cinema stars caressing their lovers, whipping both eye and thought.

Some nights she closed her eyes, believing those pictures could trouble her mind only if she saw them. But what was that beauty's condition? As soon as she closed her eyes, Kannusami stood there smiling, his eyes filled with hunger. That mental image caused more torment than the pictures. The poor woman turned upon the pillow again, right or left. Now both sides spoke messages of pleasure. What could Kalyani do?

“Kannu, can you not sleep? Why? Are mosquitoes biting?” her mother sometimes asked at night.

“Yes,” Kalyani answered. Her mother assumed that the “yes” answered her own question. It did not. Kalyani was answering the question that rose continually in her mind:

“Why struggle in vain, woman? He has entered your heart. Neither moral advice nor threats can drive him out. Did Time seek your consent before taking your husband's life? In the same way, he entered your heart without first obtaining your permission. You resisted, fought, glared as though to shoot him, and tried to frighten him. His heart neither trembled nor retreated. The more you resisted, the firmer he became. The more you pretended to ignore him, the more his love multiplied ten-thousandfold. Look, Kalyani: if that same Kannusami agrees to marry you, what obstacle have you? Do not panic and repeat the grandfather's-age tale that a woman who has lost her *thali* cannot marry. So long as a man exists to tie one, what meaning has the phrase 'lost the thali'? Now answer: would it not be sweet to marry Kannusami? Speak without concealment. Is that not what you want?”

“Yes,” Kalyani answered this inward question, while her mother went back to bed imagining that her daughter had answered whether mosquitoes were biting.

Kannusami placed a fruit in that shapely beauty's hand and, forgetting himself, touched and pulled her hand.

“What admirable conduct!” the slender-waisted woman flared.

“I did it unknowingly. Do not be angry. Hereafter...” Kannusami stammered. Before he could finish, Kalyani entered the hall. Wiping the tears brimming in his eyes, Kannusami walked outside with a frightened look. Confusion, bowed head, and anguish all struck him.

Was his longing broken? No. It crouched in hiding, ready to spring, and on another day it did spring. The circumstances could have given courage even to a coward. Chettiar was busy at the shop, while his wife had to attend the sudden death rites at a relative's home.

Kalyani wore a blue sari that day. Kannusami was no poet, but he saw the exquisite harmony between that blue sari, a face like the moon, and eyes shining like stars. She seemed a full moon in a stainless blue sky.

Having once touched her and been scorched by fire, Kannusami was somewhat shaken upon seeing her. His agitation made Kalyani herself laugh. With a teasing smile she welcomed him: “Come in, Kannusami, you sly fellow!” The smile became a tonic. He moved closer, and she shrank back a little in fear.

“Oh! A cat in the kitchen...” he cried.

“Where?” Kalyani asked, turning toward the kitchen. Summoning immense courage from somewhere, Kannusami embraced her. Cheek met cheek, then lip met lip: a joyous embrace and a rain of nectar-like kisses, all at lightning speed while Kalyani's eyes remained closed. Kannusami's body trembled with fear.

With great difficulty Kalyani freed herself. “What affliction is this? What if someone sees?” she asked in a voice compounded of a little anger, more than half fear, and for the remainder that daring which belongs to women.

At the next stage she would coo in the hall while caught in Kannusami's arms: “Mother will come. Let me go! Kannu, I hear a sound.”

“Be still, Kannu. Before she comes from the kitchen, only one more...” he would plead.

Lovers always miscount kisses. Ten becomes five and fifteen becomes nine. In the hall youth's feast was prepared, while in the kitchen Seethalakshmi Ammal remained absorbed in the silent but necessary calculation of whether the curry contained the proper measure of spice and salt.

The man who had been a bar of gold in 1938 became a “stupid donkey” in 1939. Before that year ended, Chettiar dismissed Kannusami from the shop and directed his fury at Kalyani. No one knew whence such courage came to a woman naturally frightened even by a running mouse.

“What happened has happened. Kill me, if you like, and live contentedly. Your honour must endure, must it not? Do not trouble yourself that my youth will be destroyed. Kill me, Father. Caste, family, and honour will then be protected. One woman will die, and an infant will die in her womb. Do not fear that killing a woman and killing a foetus are great sins! To these righteous men accustomed to tormenting widows, such sins are yesterday's rice. There is nothing to fear!”

“Kalyani is pregnant!” Chettiar cried, beating his head.

Within ten days of Kannusami's dismissal from the shop, Kalyani left town with him.

“She has run away! Run away! Kalyani has fled with the shop boy!” the town said.

Had Kannusami kept a diary in 1940, he would have written:

“My Inbavalli and I are well and happy. Since coming to Kanchipuram and opening a shop, I have lacked no income. My beloved wife has delivered safely. The lady doctor attended the birth with great care. I have decided to name my son Adilingam, combining the names of his two grandfathers.”

But what diary would a grocer Kannusami possess? An entry in the lady doctor's diary conveyed it:

> Child: Male  
> Condition: Healthy and beautiful  
> Father's name: Kannusami  
> Occupation: Merchant  
> Mother's name: Kalyani

In another language, the lady doctor's diary revealed what ought to have been written in Kannusami's. The municipal registrar's book of births and deaths also received this entry:

> Child, male: Name, Adilingam  
> Father's name: Kannusami  
> Mother's name: Kalyani

14 January 1945

---NOTES---
“வச்சிரம்” derives from *vajra*, denoting diamond or thunderbolt and suggesting something hard, indestructible, or powerfully striking; “A Diamond-Hard History” preserves that force while the narrative ironically constructs history from three ordinary records. The Tirukkural line is Kural 1100 and is translated for its immediate context. *Thali* is the marriage pendant whose loss conventionally marks widowhood. *Porivilangai* is retained as the name of a traditional hard sweet. The source's caste labels, moral judgement of widowhood, and sexualized descriptions belong to its characters and social critique. Clear OCR readings used in English are logged separately; the recovered first and twelfth scan pages were manually transcribed from the images into the corrected canonical source before translation.
